  "evidence": "Pinned original definitions and written proof bodies for the sign of the finite splitting, both finite differences, the zero-free contour and the quantitative application in the prime-choice stage. Source lines and names are independently reproducible using the immutable archive. The small character calculation is an independent teaching example.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "TCT-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "A single slope error for every height",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-contour-tail",
      "statement": "With D(s)=−L′(s,χ)/L(s,χ), a_n=Λ(n)χ(n), R(y)=Σ_{n≤y}a_n(y/n−1), P_Z=Σ_{n≤Z}a_n/n and S_Z(y)=Σ_{n≤Z}a_n min(1,y/n), the centered ramp equals Gχ(y)=R(y)−yD(1)=−S_Z(y)−y[D(1)−P_Z]. For h>0 the error in R(Z+h)−R(Z)−hP_Z consists precisely of new terms with Z<n≤Z+h.",
      "hypotheses": "All sums start at 1 and stop at the real endpoint rounded down. Finite splitting assumes 0<y≤Z. The increment bound requires Z≥1, h>0. The selected characters are nonprincipal of nonzero modulus.",
      "definitions": [
        "centered prime-power ramp",
        "finite clipped weight",
        "truncated slope at Z",
        "negative logarithmic derivative"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-twist-contour-split"
      ],
      "dependencies": [],
      "correspondence": "Every old term advances by ha_n/n. There are at most h+1 new terms, each of size at most log(Z+h)h/Z. For the nontrivial character modulo 3 and Z=7/2,h=3/2 the only nonzero new contribution is χ(4)Λ(4)(5/4−1)=log(2)/4. The terminal prime 5 contributes zero.",
