    {
      "id": "TCT-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The residue at one and the two contour errors",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-contour-residue",
      "statement": "Writing ΔR=R(Z+h)−R(Z), h=ηZ and σ=1−1/400+δ, there is Aδ≥0 such that |ΔR−hD(1)|≤Aδ log Q[(log Q)η Z^σ + Z²/Q]. The shift to σ at T=Q/2 crosses the kernel point s=1, with residue uD(1) for the evaluation at u.",
      "hypotheses": "0<δ≤1/800, Z≥1, 0<η≤1, Q≥2, 1≤N≤Q, χ nonprincipal modulo N, L(s,χ) nonzero for Re(s)>1−1/400, |Im(s)|≤Q. No primitivity or quadratic-order assumption is required for this estimate.",
      "definitions": [
        "Perron integral on Re(s)=2",
        "rectangular contour",
        "zero-free box",
        "logarithmic derivative with a positive horizontal margin"
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "dependencies": [],
      "correspondence": "The interior estimate bounds |D| by Cδ log Q for Re(s)≥σ, |Im(s)|≤Q−1. Near the one-line a local Landau partial fraction expansion has a weighted zero count O(log Q) and denominators of size ≥δ. Differencing the two left integrals before taking norms saves η and cancels |s|. Integrating the remaining 1/|s−1| costs O(log Q). The top and bottom edges and the tails of the right line yield the second error.",
