      "title": "Uniform cutoff for both prime-selection stages",
      "anchor": "inputs-twist-contour-budget",
      "statement": "Set x=Y^K, Z=x^400, η=x^−2, h=x^398, δ=ε/(1600K). For all sufficiently large Y satisfying Y^(ε/6)≤log Q≤Y^(ε/4), each surviving nonprincipal character satisfies x|D(1)−P_Z|≤Y^ε. A simultaneous half-budget for its finite sums then gives |Gχ(y)|≤y^(1/2+τ) throughout [Y,x].",
      "hypotheses": "The tail theorem permits 0<ε≤1, K≥1. The selected box assembly uses 0<ε≤1/4, τ>0, K≥1, the stated conductor, primitivity and zero-free conditions on χ at Q, pointwise equality of character evaluations, and a finite-sum half-budget for all heights. For the selection Q=FU and the twist supplied by the stage is primitive. Thresholds may depend on ε,K and, in the full assembly, the additional stage parameters; the tail threshold is uniform in Q,N,χ.",
